> 30 is their base level, mlevel in the relevant struct in the permonst
> array.  That gets adjusted before their actual level is set in m_lev in
> their mons struct.
>
> makemon.c::adj_lev() is what you want.  Basically, the monster's base
> level is subtracted from the difficulty of your DL.  If the result is
> negative, the monster's level is decremented by one.  If positive, the
> monster's level is increased for every 5 levels of difference.
>
> Then the monster's base level is subtracted from your XL.  For every 4
> levels that you are above the monster, the monster's level is increased
> by one.
>
> There's a hard upper bound of (1.5 * mlevel) or 49, whichever is lower,
> and a hard lower bound of 0.
>
> So for example, Death starts with a base level of 30.  The difficulty of
> your DL in the Endgame is equal to the depth of the Sanctum + 0.5*XL.
> Let's say the Sanctum was at DL:48 and you're level 26.  That makes your
> level difficulty 48+13 = 61.  The difference between that and Death's
> mlevel is positive 31, which gives us an increase of 6 levels, putting
> Death at 36.  The difference between your XL and Death's mlevel is -4,
> so no further changes are made.


